                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_credit:        `<strong>That is the diagnosis the evidence supports.</strong> The bottleneck is not megawatts — shovel-ready renewable power is cheap. It is whether ZPC can credibly pay over twenty years. Frame this as a tariff-and-creditworthiness problem and every later lever falls into place.`,
                    coach_r1_generation:    `<strong>“Build more, fast” is the seductive wrong answer.</strong> The diesel stopgap at ZMK 3.20/kWh — 3.5× the retail tariff — would blow the subsidy ceiling within a year. Private renewable power is available and cheap; the real question is whether the buyer can pay. This is not a generation problem.`,
                    coach_r1_access:        `<strong>Access cost matters, but it is not the binding constraint.</strong> The reason power is scarce and expensive is the utility’s insolvency and uncovered payment risk, not the kilometres of line. Solve creditworthiness first; the access budget then goes further.`,
                    coach_r1_silos:         `<strong>One lever per person, optimised in isolation, is exactly the trap.</strong> The bid price depends on the guarantee, the tariff depends on the bid price, and the ceiling constrains the guarantee. Power-sector reform is a system, not a checklist — design the three together.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named creditworthiness as the binding constraint, the fiscal ceiling as the hardest target, and the three levers as one interdependent system. That framing is what lets the auction, tariff and guarantee reinforce rather than fight each other.`,
                    coach_r2_realistic:     `<strong>A realistic discovery ceiling is the disciplined move.</strong> Set above credible build cost, it lets the market reveal the true price instead of dictating it. With your credit package the premium is contained and the auction fills with credible bidders.`,
                    coach_r2_undersubscribed: `<strong>Bid day arrives — two bids for 90 MW against a 600 MW tender.</strong> Under-subscribed by 85%. A reserve set as a political statement, below what a credible developer can build for, is the single most common African auction failure Eberhard documents. You have burned ten months and signalled that Zambeka sets unrealistic ceilings. A reserve price is a discovery instrument, not a slogan.`,
                    coach_r2_high:          `<strong>It will certainly fill — and ratepayers will overpay for it.</strong> A ceiling that generous removes the competitive tension that drives the price down. You have bought certainty with money that belonged to consumers. A realistic ceiling above build cost would have cleared lower.`,
                    coach_r2_nocredit:      `<strong>You declined the guarantee to protect the ceiling — and the bids priced a payment-risk premium instead.</strong> The winning price came in roughly ZMK 0.40/kWh higher than it needed to. Over twenty years that premium costs ratepayers more than the guarantee ever would have. Uncovered offtaker risk is paid for either way — visibly, or invisibly through higher tariffs.`,
                    coach_r2_allvolume:     `<strong>Tendering the full 600 MW at once forgoes a learned price.</strong> A credible first tranche builds a pipeline and lets a second round clear lower as developers gain confidence in Zambeka. Decisive on paper; more expensive in practice.`,
                    coach_r2_token:         `<strong>A 150 MW pilot is safe and barely moves the gap.</strong> You will reach the 24-month review with most of the 600 MW still untendered and the diesel bills still running. A credible first tranche is bigger than a token.`,
                    coach_r3_glide:         `<strong>The glide-path reaches cost-reflectivity by Year 3 and survives the politics.</strong> Four months after a contested by-election, a phased path with a lifeline block gets you to the same balance sheet without handing the Minister a reason to reverse you. Sequencing is the political economy of reform.`,
                    coach_r3_shock:         `<strong>You moved straight to cost-reflectivity — technically correct, politically explosive.</strong> Consumer groups are in the streets and the Minister is signalling a rollback. If the tariff is reversed under pressure you lose both the revenue and your credibility as an independent regulator. Is there a glide-path that reaches the same place by Year 3?`,
                    coach_r3_shocklifeline: `<strong>The lifeline block just saved your shock from a rollback.</strong> Protecting poor households’ first consumption block gives the increase the political room to survive — even a sharp move is defensible when the most vulnerable are shielded. Distribution is what makes cost-reflectivity politically durable.`,
                    coach_r3_freeze:        `<strong>Freezing the tariff leaves the gap and the subsidy exactly where they are.</strong> ZPC stays insolvent, the offtaker stays sub-investment-grade, and every future bid still prices that risk. Affordability is real, but a frozen tariff is not affordability — it is deferred insolvency.`,
                    coach_r3_gridonly:      `<strong>You poured the access budget into grid extension at ZMK 9,500 a connection.</strong> The budget reaches a fraction of your target households before it runs out — the rest could have been reached by mini-grids at ZMK 3,200. You optimised for the word “grid” in the directive over the access number the directive actually demands.`,
                    coach_r3_leastcost:     `<strong>That is least-cost electrification.</strong> Blending grid extension with off-grid and mini-grid systems stretches every kwacha toward the access number, instead of chasing the “grid” word. The same budget reaches far more households.`,
                    coach_r3_strong:        `<strong>A coherent tariff-and-access package.</strong> A glide-path with a lifeline block restores solvency without inviting a rollback, a real off-grid blend reaches the households at least cost, and performance-based regulation pays ZPC to improve rather than to exist. The three reinforce each other.`,
                    coach_r4_shell:         `<strong>The shell fills your volume — and never delivers a watt.</strong> No plant, no financing, a politically exposed owner. The headline says you “closed the auction”; in 24 months the lights are still off and your credibility is gone. Qualification criteria protect delivery, not paperwork — and the public will remember the lights, not the press release.`,
                    coach_r4_shelldq:       `<strong>Disqualifying the shell is the call that protects delivery.</strong> You take lower volume this round rather than award a project that never produces power. That is what bidder qualification is for. The megawatts you contract now are megawatts that will actually reach the grid.`,
                    coach_r4_protest:       `<strong>Conceding the rollback keeps the peace and loses the reform.</strong> The revenue evaporates, the subsidy widens, and you have taught every future Minister that ZERA folds under pressure. An independent regulator that reverses on the street is no longer independent.`,
                    coach_r4_protesthold:   `<strong>Holding the glide-path while accelerating the lifeline is the durable response.</strong> You defend the reform without abandoning the vulnerable — and you keep your independence. Communicate the why, shield the poorest, and let the path stand.`,
                    coach_r4_drought:       `<strong>Firing up diesel breaches the ceiling — exactly the trap.</strong> Emergency diesel at ZMK 3.20/kWh blows the ZMK 4.1bn limit and undoes the fiscal discipline the whole plan rests on. The shock is real, but diesel is the most expensive answer to it.`,
                    coach_r4_droughtrenew:  `<strong>Bringing forward auctioned renewables and storage protects the ceiling.</strong> You answer the hydro shortfall with the cheap capacity you already procured rather than expensive diesel. The reform absorbs the shock instead of being broken by it.`,
                    coach_r4_capacityonly:  `<strong>“We procured power” is the answer that hides the risk.</strong> Megawatts contracted at an unbankable price, or to a bidder that cannot deliver, are not power. A donor panel will ask the next question: at what price, and will it actually be built?`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Leading with the one flattering number is how panels stop trusting regulators.</strong> They can see capacity, access, price and the subsidy together. Emphasise the favourable one and downplay the rest, and they will assume the rest is worse than it is.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Named creditworthiness as the constraint; treated the levers as one system.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Misread the constraint or split the levers apart.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Realistic reserve, credit enhancement and robust qualification — the auction cleared.`,
                    sum_r2_failed:          `Reserve set too low — the auction under-subscribed.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Auction cleared but left a premium or a delivery risk on the table.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Glide-path with lifeline, least-cost access blend and performance-based regulation.`,
                    sum_r3_rollback:        `Full tariff shock — politically reversed.`,
                    sum_r3_gridonly:        `Poured the access budget into grid extension.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Tariff and access plan only partly reconciled.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Held the reform through the shock; defended the integrated balance; staked delivered MW.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Folded to the shock, chased megawatts, or staked the wrong metric.`,

                    insight_powered:        `Grid powered: you ran the genuine reform pattern. A realistic reserve and a credible credit-enhancement package cleared the auction at a bankable price; a phased glide-path with a lifeline restored solvency without a rollback; a least-cost grid-and-off-grid blend stretched the access budget; and you held the reform through the live shock. Capacity, access and affordability all advanced within the ZMK 4.1bn ceiling.`,
                    insight_auctionfailed:  `Auction failure — the headline error: a reserve price set as a political statement, below credible build cost, under-subscribed the tender by the bulk of its volume. Eberhard’s data names this the single most common African auction failure. You burned 9–12 months, capped your delivered capacity, and signalled to every developer that Zambeka sets unrealistic ceilings.`,
                    insight_riskuncovered:  `Uncovered offtaker risk: skipping credit enhancement to protect the fiscal ceiling did not save money — the winning bids priced a payment-risk premium of roughly ZMK 0.40/kWh instead. Over twenty years that costs ratepayers more than the guarantee would have. The risk is paid either way: visibly via a guarantee or invisibly via a higher tariff.`,
                    insight_rollback:       `Tariff reversed: a full 30% shock months after a contested by-election triggered a politically driven rollback. You lost both the revenue and your credibility as an independent regulator. A phased glide-path with a lifeline block would have reached the same cost-reflectivity and survived the politics.`,
                    insight_gridonly:       `Grid-only access: you optimised for the word “grid” over the access number the directive actually demanded. At ZMK 9,500 per connection the budget ran out well short of target; mini-grids at ZMK 3,200 would have reached many more households per kwacha. Least-cost electrification blends both pathways.`,
                    insight_shell:          `Lights still off: awarding the politically connected shell filled the volume on paper but delivered no power. Bidder qualification exists to protect delivery, not paperwork — and in 24 months the public remembers the lights, not the press release closing the auction.`,
                    insight_breach:         `Ceiling breached: the subsidy drawn exceeded the ZMK 4.1bn limit, failing the one hard constraint the Cabinet directive set. Whatever capacity or access you bought, breaching the fiscal ceiling is the failure mode the directive was written to prevent.`,
                    insight_silos:          `You treated the three levers as independent workstreams. The auction, the tariff and the guarantee interlock — the bid price depends on the guarantee, the tariff depends on the bid price, the ceiling constrains the guarantee — and optimising them in isolation left value on the table.`,
                    insight_misdiagnosed:   `You never fully named the binding constraint as tariff and creditworthiness, so the plan kept reaching for megawatts when the real bottleneck was whether the utility could credibly pay. The diagnosis is the decision that makes every other decision.`,
                    insight_partial:        `A partial reform: parts of the plan landed, but the auction, tariff, credit and access choices did not fully reinforce each other, leaving the Cabinet targets only partly met and the programme’s credibility short of bankable.`,

                    hint_auctionfailed:     `Your auction under-subscribed — the reserve price was set below credible build cost. Re-tender at a realistic ceiling above build cost and pair it with credit enhancement before the diesel bills compound.`,
                    hint_shell:             `Awarding the shell bidder leaves you with contracted MW that will never reach the grid. Qualification criteria protect delivery — the megawatts that count are the ones that get built.`,
                    hint_breach:            `You have breached the ZMK 4.1bn subsidy ceiling — the one hard constraint in the directive. Bring forward auctioned renewables instead of diesel and let cost-reflective tariffs and performance-based regulation close the gap.`,
                    hint_riskuncovered:     `You skipped credit enhancement, so bidders are pricing a payment-risk premium into every winning bid. The premium costs ratepayers more than the guarantee would. Add an escrow or partial risk guarantee.`,
                    hint_rollback:          `The tariff shock was reversed under political pressure — you lost the revenue and your independence. Sequence a glide-path with a lifeline block that reaches cost-reflectivity by Year 3.`,
                    hint_gridonly:          `Your access budget went almost entirely into grid extension at ZMK 9,500/connection. Blend in mini-grids at ZMK 3,200 to reach far more households toward the 60% target.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — the auction cleared bankably, the ceiling holds and credibility is high. Land it: defend the integrated balance and stake the delivered MW, not the headline.`,

